  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0164 database is designed to manage data for a retail business, specifically a coffee shop. It contains nine tables that work together to store information about customers, products, sales, and staff. The Customer table holds details about each customer, including their ID, home store, name, email, date of birth, loyalty card number, and birth year. The Generation table is used to categorize customers by their birth year into different generations. The Product table stores information about each product sold, such as its ID, group, category, type, description, unit of measure, wholesale and retail prices, and whether it's tax-exempt or on promotion. The Date table contains information about dates, including the date, week, month, quarter, and year, with each date assigned a unique ID. The Pastry_Inventory table tracks the sales of pastries, including the sales outlet, transaction date, product ID, quantity sold, and waste. The Sales_Target table sets sales targets for each sales outlet by year and month, including goals for beans, beverages, food, merchandise, and total sales. The Sales_Outlet table stores information about each sales outlet, including its ID, type, square footage, address, city, state, telephone number, postal code, longitude, latitude, manager, and neighborhood. The Staff table holds details about each staff member, including their ID, first and last name, position, start date, and location. The Sales_Receipt table tracks each transaction, including the transaction ID, date, time, sales outlet ID, staff ID, customer ID, whether the sale was made in-store or not, the order number, line item ID, product ID, quantity sold, line item amount, unit price, and whether the item was on promotion.",
  "gold_question": "Background / Context\n\nThe coffee industry has experienced significant changes due to the COVID-19 pandemic, with reduced in-store visitors but increased demand for coffee delivery and takeaway to support people working from home. This situation necessitates a robust approach to managing operational data, specifically through implementing a data warehouse to optimize operations during various business hours.\n\n Objectives\n\n- Design a data warehouse dimensional model for analyzing operating hours in the coffee industry.\n- Enhance decision-making regarding sales, inventory, and customer engagement.\n\n Stakeholders & Users\n\n- Coffee Shop Owners/Managers: Requires insights into operations, sales, and inventory.\n- Data Analysts: Utilize the data warehouse to extract meaningful insights.\n- Operational Staff: Manage sales outlets and customer engagement efficiently.\n\n Functional Descriptions\n\n1. Customer Management: Maintain comprehensive customer profiles including loyalty and preferences.\n2. Product Tracking: Categorize and manage products with detailed information on pricing, promotions, and descriptions.\n3. Sales and Inventory Analysis: Track daily sales, inventory changes, waste management, and targets across outlets.\n4. Sales Outlet Management: Capture detailed outlet information including location, size, and management details.\n5. Staff Management: Maintain staff information related to roles, locations, and start dates.\n6. Transaction Handling: Record sales receipts and transaction details across outlets, capturing in-store and online sales.\n\n Non-Functional Descriptions\n\n- Scalability: Support expansion to accommodate more outlets and increased data volume.\n- Security: Protect sensitive customer and financial data.\n- Data Accuracy: Ensure high data integrity and minimal errors in reporting.\n- Performance: Enable fast data retrieval and report generation even under heavy load.\n- Reliability: Maintain consistent data updates and system uptime to support seamless operations.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0164.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0164\nCREATE TABLE `customer` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `home_store` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `date_of_birth` date NOT NULL,\n  `loyalty_card_number` varchar(50) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `birth_year` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `generation` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `start_year` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `end_year` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `product` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `group` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `category` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `description` text DEFAULT NULL,\n  `unit_of_measure` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `wholesale_price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `retail_price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `tax_exempt` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  `on_promotion` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `date` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `date` date NOT NULL,\n  `week` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `month` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `quarter` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `year` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `pastry_inventory` (\n  `sales_outlet` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `transaction_date` date NOT NULL,\n  `product_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `quantity_sold` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `waste` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`product_id`) REFERENCES `product`(`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `sales_target` (\n  `sales_outlet` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `year` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `month` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `goal_beans` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `goal_beverages` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `goal_food` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `goal_merchandise` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `goal_total_sales` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`sales_outlet`, `year`, `month`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `sales_outlet` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `square_footage` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `city` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `state` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `telephone_number` varchar(15) NOT NULL,\n  `postal_code` varchar(10) NOT NULL,\n  `longitude` decimal(10,8) NOT NULL,\n  `latitude` decimal(10,8) NOT NULL,\n  `manager` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `neighborhood` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `staff` (\n  `id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `first_name` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `last_name` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `position` varchar(100) NOT NULL,\n  `start_date` date NOT NULL,\n  `location` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `sales_receipt` (\n  `transaction_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `date` date NOT NULL,\n  `time` time NOT NULL,\n  `sales_outlet_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `staff_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `customer_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `in_store` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 1,\n  `order_number` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `line_item_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `product_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `quantity_sold` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `line_item_amount` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `unit_price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `on_promotion` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`transaction_id`),\n  FOREIGN KEY (`sales_outlet_id`) REFERENCES `sales_outlet`(`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`staff_id`) REFERENCES `staff`(`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`customer_id`) REFERENCES `customer`(`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  FOREIGN KEY (`product_id`) REFERENCES `product`(`id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
